The main contribution is that it shows measurement-based quantum computation can be made universal with weaker/simpler resources than previously thought, using only projective measurements rather than more general measurement operations.

In essence, the result demonstrates that a suitably chosen entangled many-body state can still serve as a universal resource for quantum computation even when the allowed measurements are restricted to standard projective measurements. This lowers the experimental and theoretical resource requirements for MBQC, because projective measurements are generally simpler to implement than arbitrary POVMs or more complex adaptive operations.

So the significance is:

- it reduces the measurement complexity needed for universal MBQC;
- it identifies a more experimentally accessible route to quantum computation;
- it strengthens the understanding of which entangled states are genuinely universal resources;
- and it narrows the gap between abstract MBQC models and physically realizable quantum systems.
